Let \(X\) and \(Y\) be continuous random variables with probability density functions \(P_X(x)\) and \(P_Y(y)\), respectively. Further, let \(Y=X^2\) and \[P_X(x)=
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; x \in (0,1] \\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}\] Which one of the following options is correct?

questions.explanation:
Given, \[P_X(x)=
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; x\in(0,1] \\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}\] and \[Y=X^2\] Since \(x\in(0,1]\), we have \[y=x^2 \quad \Rightarrow \quad x=\sqrt{y}\] Using transformation of random variables, \[P_Y(y)=P_X(x)\left|\frac{dx}{dy}\right|\] Now, \[\frac{dx}{dy}=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{y}}\] Also, \[P_X(x)=1\] Hence, \[P_Y(y)=1\cdot \frac{1}{2\sqrt{y}}\] Therefore, \[P_Y(y)=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{y}}, &amp; y\in(0,1] \\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) $P_Y(y)=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{y}}, &amp; y \in (0,1] \\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}$}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For one-to-one transformation \(Y=g(X)\), \[P_Y(y)=P_X(x)\left|\dfrac{dx}{dy}\right|\]</li><li>If \(Y=X^2\) and \(X&gt;0\), then \(x=\sqrt{y}\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not forget the Jacobian term \(\left|\dfrac{dx}{dy}\right|\).</li><li>Ensure the support changes from \(x\in(0,1]\) to \(y\in(0,1]\).</li></ul></div>

