A stable first-order LTI system has the unit step response \[y(t)=K\left(1-e^{-t/\tau}\right),\] where \(K\) is the steady-state value and \(\tau\) is the time constant. From the table, \[K=y(\infty)=3.\] At one time constant, \[y(\tau)=0.632\,K=0.632\times3=1.896.\] From the given data, \[y(1.6)=1.65,\qquad
y(2.6)=2.18.\] Since \(1.896\) lies much closer to the output at \(t=2.6\,\text{s}\), the time constant is approximately \[\tau\approx2\,\text{s}.\] Using \[3\left(1-e^{-t/\tau}\right)=2.18,\] we get \[e^{-2.6/\tau}=1-\frac{2.18}{3}=0.2733.\] Hence, \[\tau=\frac{2.6}{-\ln(0.2733)}
\approx1.99\,\text{s}.\] Thus, \[\tau\approx2\,\text{s}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }2}\]

questions.question:
Consider a discrete-time linear time-invariant (LTI) system \(S\), where \[y[n]=S\{x[n]\}\] Let \[S\{\delta[n]\}=
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; n\in\{0,1,2\} \\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}\] where \(\delta[n]\) is the discrete-time unit impulse function. For an input signal \(x[n]\), the output \(y[n]\) is

questions.explanation:
For a discrete-time LTI system, the output is given by the convolution of the input with the impulse response: \[y[n]=x[n]*h[n],\] where \[h[n]=S\{\delta[n]\}=
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; n\in\{0,1,2\},\\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div>The impulse response can be written as \[h[n]=\delta[n]+\delta[n-1]+\delta[n-2].\] Hence, \[\begin{aligned}
y[n]
&amp;=x[n]*h[n] \\
&amp;=x[n]*\left(\delta[n]+\delta[n-1]+\delta[n-2]\right) \\
&amp;=x[n]+x[n-1]+x[n-2].
\end{aligned}\] Therefore, the correct expression matches Option A.</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div>Using the convolution sum, \[y[n]=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}x[k]\,h[n-k].\] Since \(h[n-k]\) is non-zero only for \[n-k=0,1,2,\] we have \[k=n,\;n-1,\;n-2.\] Thus, \[y[n]=x[n]+x[n-1]+x[n-2].\]</div> \[\boxed{\text{(a) }x[n]+x[n-1]+x[n-2]}\]

