Given, \[\rho=
\begin{cases}
-3\rho_0, &amp; 0\le R\le a\\
0, &amp; R&gt;a
\end{cases}
\qquad (\rho_0&gt;0)\] <strong>For</strong> \(0\le R\le a\): Using Gauss's law, \[\oint \vec E\cdot d\vec S=\frac{Q_{\text{enc}}}{\varepsilon_0}\] For a Gaussian sphere of radius \(R\), \[Q_{\text{enc}}=(-3\rho_0)\left(\frac{4}{3}\pi R^3\right)=-4\pi\rho_0R^3\] So, \[E(4\pi R^2)=\frac{-4\pi\rho_0R^3}{\varepsilon_0}
\Rightarrow
E_R=-\frac{\rho_0R}{\varepsilon_0}\] Thus, \[E_R=-\frac{\rho_0R}{\varepsilon_0},\qquad 0\le R\le a\] At \(R=a\), \[E_R(a)=-\frac{\rho_0a}{\varepsilon_0}\] <strong>For</strong> \(R&gt;a\): Total charge enclosed is \[Q=(-3\rho_0)\left(\frac{4}{3}\pi a^3\right)=-4\pi\rho_0a^3\] Again by Gauss's law, \[E(4\pi R^2)=\frac{-4\pi\rho_0a^3}{\varepsilon_0}
\Rightarrow
E_R=-\frac{\rho_0a^3}{\varepsilon_0R^2}\] Thus, \[E_R=-\frac{\rho_0a^3}{\varepsilon_0R^2},\qquad R&gt;a\] At \(R=a\), \[E_R(a)=-\frac{\rho_0a^3}{\varepsilon_0a^2}
=-\frac{\rho_0a}{\varepsilon_0}\] So the electric field is continuous at \(R=a\). Hence, the graph is: i) linear for \(R&lt;a\), ii) varies as \(-1/R^2\) for \(R&gt;a\), iii) and tends to zero as \(R\to\infty\). <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> Therefore, the correct plot is Fig. (iii). \[\boxed{C}\]

